<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Ijebu Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1400 CE – 1892 CE (approx.) · capital Ijebu-Ode</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Yoruba coastal kingdom east of Lagos whose rulers, the Awujale, grew rich as middlemen between the lagoon ports and the interior.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ijebu tradition names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obanta">Obanta</a> as the founder who came from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ife">Ile-Ife</a> and settled at Ijebu-Ode. The area was already densely settled: the vast earthwork known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sungbos-eredo">Sungbo's Eredo</a>, a ditch and bank around 160 km long, was dug around the end of the first millennium. The kingdom took shape by the 15th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awujale">Awujale</a> ruled with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osugbo">Osugbo</a> society of elders, which held judicial power, and with town chiefs. Ijebu controlled routes between the lagoon and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> interior and traded cloth, kola and slaves with Europeans on the coast. In the 19th century it profited from the wars between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ibadan">Ibadan</a> and its rivals while restricting access to its markets.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When Ijebu closed its roads to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lagos">Lagos</a> traders, a British force defeated its army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-imagbon">Imagbon</a> in 1892 and occupied Ijebu-Ode. The kingdom was absorbed into British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> and later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/protectorate-of-southern-nigeria">Protectorate of Southern Nigeria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awujale">Awujale</a> still reigns at Ijebu-Ode.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ijebu-kingdom.jpg" alt="Ijebu Kingdom" width="320" height="418"><figcaption>Image: Source: Henry B. Lovejoy, “Re-Drawing Historical Maps of the Bight of Benin Hinterland, c. 1780.” Canadian Journal of... · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oyo_Empire_at_Its_Greatest_Extent,_c._1780_(5).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1400 CE – 1892 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ijebu-Ode</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Obanta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Yoruba religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yoruba</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ijebu, Ijebu-Ode</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1646&amp;focus=ijebu-kingdom" aria-label="Ijebu Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4893 1833 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ijebu Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M5094 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5213 2089h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5213 2089h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Nigeria. <a href="/atlas?year=1646&amp;focus=ijebu-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

